## Summary

`just clippy` (`cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`) exits **101 with 65
errors on master `cb28c49`**. It is not a regression from any one lane — it is
red for everyone, so every lane that runs it either skips it or learns to read
past a failing gate, which is the worse outcome.

Measured 2026-08-31 from a clean detached worktree at `cb28c49`
(`CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/xertrov/.cache/lsp-openplanet-builds/b074-baseline`):

```
master_just_clippy_exit=101
n_errors=65
```

Without `-D warnings` the same invocation exits 0 and prints 83 `warning:`
lines (40 unique after normalising `file:LINE:COL`). So the recipe's `-D
warnings` is the only thing separating green from red — the lints themselves
have been accumulating quietly.

## Breakdown (master `cb28c49`)

| n | lint |
|---|---|
| 20 | expression creates a reference immediately dereferenced (`needless_borrow`) |
| 10 | very complex type; consider a `type` alias (`type_complexity`) |
| 6 | unneeded `return` |
| 6 | `if` collapsible into the outer `match` |
| 4 | redundant closure |
| 3 | **unused import** — `super::repr::ParamMode`, `crate::testing::TempDir`, `crate::parser::ast::SourceFile` |
| 2 | explicit lifetimes could be elided |
| 2 | field assignment outside initializer for `Default::default()` |
| 12 | one each: `values()` iteration, `&mut Vec` vs slice, useless `format!`, `x - 1 >=`, derivable `impl`, `?` operator, constant-value assertion, loop index, module named as its parent, empty line after doc comment |

The three **unused imports** are the ones worth reading first: they are rustc
warnings, not style lints, and they mark code that a recent change left behind.
`crate::testing::TempDir` in `src/typedb/index.rs` and
`crate::parser::ast::SourceFile` both appear in the ordinary `cargo test` build
output too, so they are visible on every build in this repo right now.

## Why this matters beyond tidiness

A gate that is red for reasons unrelated to your change cannot tell you whether
your change is clean. The workaround every lane is forced into — a
line-normalised set diff of branch-vs-master clippy output — is a good
technique but it is a *substitute* for the gate, and it needs a master baseline
run each time (about 2 minutes of build). Making `just clippy` green once
restores a cheap boolean.

## Suggested work

1. Fix the 3 unused imports first — smallest, and they are real dead code.
2. Sweep the mechanical lints (`needless_borrow`, `needless_return`,
   `redundant_closure`, `collapsible_if`) with `cargo clippy --fix`, reviewing
   the diff rather than trusting it.
3. Decide deliberately on `type_complexity` (10 sites): either introduce the
   aliases or `#![allow]` it crate-wide with a one-line reason. Do not leave it
   as the reason the gate stays red.
4. Re-run `just clippy` and confirm exit 0, then keep it in whatever CI or
   pre-merge check exists so it cannot silently rot again.

## Provenance

Found by lane-b065-b066 while gating B074. B074 itself adds **zero** clippy
findings: the line-normalised set diff between branch `b074-env-enforcement`
and master `cb28c49` is empty in both directions, and both trees report the
same 65 errors under `-D warnings`. Filed rather than fixed, per the standing
instruction not to widen a ticket.